WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.320
An Oscar winning dame's high-octane dream. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:07.560
world-famous driver's subtle cameo.&nbsp;
And what's Cardi B doing bailing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:11.280
out Vin Diesel? Here's everything you&nbsp;
missed in the "Fast &amp; Furious" films.

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:16.860
The "Fast &amp; Furious" franchise has come a&nbsp;
long way since its debut in 2001. The story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:20.340
in the first film, "The Fast and the&nbsp;
Furious," comes from humble beginnings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:24.240
as it follows the journey of undercover&nbsp;
cop Brian O'Connor, played by the late,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:28.740
great Paul Walker. The plot details how Brian&nbsp;
infiltrates a group of competitive street&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:33.660
racers run by Vin Diesel's character, Dominic&nbsp;
Toretto, better known as Dom. For the most part,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:37.560
things seem to be going well for the&nbsp;
young LAPD officer, aside from the fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:43.020
that Dom's right-hand man, Vince, mistrusts&nbsp;
Brian. He clocks him as a cop right away.

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:48.060
Brian runs into further complications when&nbsp;
he forms a true bond with Dom and begins&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:52.500
developing feelings for his new friend's&nbsp;
younger sister, Mia. When Brian comes to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:57.180
stop Dom and his crew from a car theft gone&nbsp;
wrong, he helps rescue Vince who's trapped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:01.560
on a truck. Unfortunately, Vince gets caught up&nbsp;
in a wire that's cutting into his arm. Luckily,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:05.820
Brian is able to save him, and when Vince&nbsp;
returns to the franchise in "Fast Five"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:11.400
the scars from that ordeal are present on his&nbsp;
arm. This small detail goes a long way to help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:16.560
encourage a sense of consistency in a franchise&nbsp;
that doesn't always have the best continuity.

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:20.220
"Are you serious right now? Man, this is crazy!"

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:24.120
By the end of "The Fast and the Furious,"&nbsp;
Brian has put his career in jeopardy by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:27.240
helping out Dom and his crew. He&nbsp;
ultimately lets Dom go in order&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:30.480
to stop him from getting arrested.&nbsp;
Audiences learn in the next film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:36.120
"2 Fast 2 Furious," that this decision leads&nbsp;
to Brian losing his job as an LAPD officer.

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:41.100
"Fast &amp; Furious 6" really amps up the&nbsp;
change of direction in the franchise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:45.780
The familiar crew of street racers has now&nbsp;
turned into the only hope to stop a criminal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:50.280
organization run by Owen Shaw, played by&nbsp;
Luke Evans. Dwayne Johnson's character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:55.080
Diplomatic Security Service agent Hobbs, who&nbsp;
once hunted Dom and his crew, has now turned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:59.340
to them for help in assisting with the other&nbsp;
crew's arrest. According to Hobbs, the crew is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:05.220
similar to Dom's and has even enlisted the help of&nbsp;
Dom's old girlfriend Letty, who was presumed dead.

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:09.660
By the end of the film, all is well for&nbsp;
the furious family. Hobbs has granted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:13.020
the entire crew pardons from the U.S.&nbsp;
government, allowing them to return to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:16.740
the home where the franchise first began.&nbsp;
Letty has joined their team once more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:20.940
and while she still suffers from memory&nbsp;
loss, she and Dom are back together.

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:23.760
At the end of the film, Tyrese Gibson's character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:27.360
Pearce, has a brief exchange with Hobbs&nbsp;
when he appears at their barbeque.

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:31.620
"Hey Mia, better hide your&nbsp;
baby oil — I'm just playing."

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:33.240
"You'd better hide that big-ass forehead."

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:35.010
"I was just joking, whatever."

00:02:35.010 --> 00:02:38.400
As it turns out, Hobbs' crack at&nbsp;
Pearce was improved by Johnson.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:42.002
The scene was kept in the final&nbsp;
cut due to its hilarious timing.

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:47.100
It's no secret that Dwayne Johnson is one of the&nbsp;
hardest-working entertainers in the business. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:51.720
fact, according to Forbes, he was one of the&nbsp;
highest-paid actors in 2022 due to his roles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:56.640
in the Furious franchise, "Black Adam," and&nbsp;
"Red Notice" to name a few. However, before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:02.400
he made a name for himself in Hollywood, Johnson&nbsp;
made waves with his wrestling persona: The Rock.

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:07.020
It seems as though professional wrestling is&nbsp;
a family business for Johnson, as his father,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:10.260
Rocky, was a famous Canadian wrestler. Similarly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:14.040
his eldest daughter Simone has recently&nbsp;
announced that she's following in her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:19.320
family's famous footsteps. She has debuted her&nbsp;
wrestling name to be Ava Raine, according to TMZ.

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:23.940
At the beginning of "Furious 7," audiences are&nbsp;
introduced to the latest threat to the Furious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:29.640
family, Owen Shaw's older brother, Deckard&nbsp;
Shaw. Shaw is out for vengeance after Hobbs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:35.040
Dom, and his crew put a stop to his brother's&nbsp;
activities, placing Owen in a coma in the process.

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:40.080
Shaw and Hobbs engage in an epic fight when Shaw&nbsp;
breaks into the Diplomatic Security Service,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:45.240
or DSS, office in Los Angeles. During the&nbsp;
fight, Johnson shows that he has retained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:50.040
many of his hand-to-hand combat skills from his&nbsp;
previous career. Longtime fans of "The Rock"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:55.920
might even recognize when he uses his classic&nbsp;
finisher, "The Rock Bottom," against Shaw, a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:00.180
detail surely included as a nod to those who have&nbsp;
followed the entertainer since his early days.

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:05.520
The "Fast and Furious" spinoff film, "Hobbs&nbsp;
&amp; Shaw," digs deeper into the Shaw family's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:09.960
story. The movie introduces the character&nbsp;
Hattie Shaw, the younger sister of Deckard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:15.660
and Owen. She is an MI6 field agent that is being&nbsp;
relentlessly hunted by terrorist Brixton Lore.

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:20.040
However, before Hobbs learns any of this,&nbsp;
he spends some time interrogating her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:23.760
as he believes that she's a criminal.&nbsp;
At the end of his interrogation, he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:26.160
quotes the German philosopher Friedrich Nietzsche.

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:29.640
"There's more wisdom in your body&nbsp;
than in your deepest philosophies."

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:32.160
"Oh let me guess: is that Bruce Lee?"

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:35.040
"Hahaha, no — Nietzche."

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:39.660
This is much to the surprise of Hattie, who had&nbsp;
assumed that he was not enlightened enough to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:43.920
discuss such a celebrated intellectual.&nbsp;
However, the joke is clearly on her.

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:48.540
This might have seemed like it was a tool used&nbsp;
in order to give Hattie and Hobbs some sexual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:52.620
tension, but there's a smaller detail that&nbsp;
foreshadows his interest in Nietzsche. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:57.180
beginning of the film outlines the morning routine&nbsp;
that Hobbs goes through in contrast with Shaw. One&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.180 --> 00:05:01.320
moment showcases the two making breakfast,&nbsp;
which has a blink-and-you-miss-it moment.

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:05.160
Hobbs is making eggs in a bowl, where&nbsp;
audiences can see a copy of the book&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:09.660
"The Portable Nietzsche" next to his stove. The&nbsp;
film already told us that he's a Nietzsche fan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:15.293
but it was only visible to those who were paying&nbsp;
attention to those seemingly-minute details.

00:05:15.293 --> 00:05:18.628
"That's good. I'm impressed."

00:05:18.628 --> 00:05:24.360
"F9" was released in 2021 and saw the return of&nbsp;
director Justin Lin to the franchise. Currently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:28.500
Lin has directed a total of five "Furious"&nbsp;
films: "The Fast and the Furious:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:34.380
Tokyo Drift," "Fast &amp; Furious," "Fast&nbsp;
Five," "Fast &amp; Furious 6," and "F9."

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:39.420
The story in "F9" doubles down on the core&nbsp;
concept of family within the franchise as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:43.200
it's revealed that Dom and Mia have a&nbsp;
brother that has, up until this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:47.520
never been mentioned. Their brother&nbsp;
Jakob Toretto is played by John Cena.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:51.900
He is estranged from his family after Dom&nbsp;
discovers that he is partially responsible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:56.340
for the death of their father. Years later,&nbsp;
Jakob returns for revenge against Dom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.340 --> 00:06:01.980
as he's become an renowned criminal working as&nbsp;
a thief, assassin, and high-performance driver.

00:06:01.980 --> 00:06:07.440
The start of the film uses an older logo for&nbsp;
Universal studios. In the film's commentary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:12.000
Lin details that this was an intentional&nbsp;
decision made on his part. He points out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:14.700
that the film begins with a&nbsp;
flashback to Dom's father's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.700 --> 00:06:18.600
death in 1989 to provide context&nbsp;
for the Toretto boys' relationship.

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:21.720
In the original "The Fast and&nbsp;
the Furious," Dom recounts how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:26.220
he physically beat the man responsible&nbsp;
for his father's death. In "F9," we also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.220 --> 00:06:29.400
see a flashback of the assault.&nbsp;
In the commentary, Lin explained,

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:31.860
"The choice of going with an older logo was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:35.580
because I wanted it to sync up&nbsp;
with this 'flashback' to start."

00:06:36.540 --> 00:06:41.400
"F9" brings Helen Mirren's return to the main&nbsp;
Furious franchise after her starring role in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:46.560
"Hobbs and Shaw." She portrays the Shaw siblings&nbsp;
Deckard, Owen, and presumably Hattie's mother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:50.940
Queenie. When Queenie is first introduced,&nbsp;
audiences see that being a criminal seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:54.360
to run in the Shaw family, as she's&nbsp;
shown attempting to steal a necklace.

00:06:54.360 --> 00:06:59.040
When Dom approaches her, she provides him with&nbsp;
information on his brother, Jakob Toretto's,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:03.420
location. During their conversation, Queenie is&nbsp;
shown driving recklessly through the streets of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:08.460
London. As street racing is the origin of the&nbsp;
"Furious" franchise, this decision was likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:13.500
made to keep in the tradition of being too&nbsp;
fast and too furious. However, it was also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:18.600
a special request made by the actress herself.&nbsp;
According to Yahoo! Entertainment, Mirren shared,

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:24.300
"My great ambition is to be in a Fast&nbsp;
and Furious movie. I so want to be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:28.620
mad driver in a Fast and Furious movie.&nbsp;
My claim to fame is I always do my own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:32.924
driving — I was on Top Gear, and I&nbsp;
did [my lap] in a very good time."

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:39.000
Rapper Cardi B appears in a small guest role in&nbsp;
"F9" as Leysa, a woman who shares history with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:43.320
Dom. Her character rescues him from being&nbsp;
arrested by posing as a police officer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.320 --> 00:07:46.320
and provides him with a key to his&nbsp;
freedom once they're put in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:50.280
back of a police van. While her connection&nbsp;
with Dom may have seemed random at first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.280 --> 00:07:54.780
she does have a seemingly throw-away line that&nbsp;
holds hidden weight to who her character is.

00:07:54.780 --> 00:08:00.360
In the film, she says that if it wasn't for&nbsp;
Dom, she would still be stealing gas. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:04.440
is a direct connection to Dom, Letty,&nbsp;
and Han's time living in the Dominican&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.440 --> 00:08:09.300
Republic. In the fourth film of the franchise,&nbsp;
"Fast &amp; Furious," those three characters live&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:13.200
in the Dominican stealing gas to make a&nbsp;
living. Their time here takes place after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:18.180
the events of the first film, when Brian&nbsp;
lets Dom go rather than have him arrested.

00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:23.340
As he and Letty become wanted criminals, they&nbsp;
leave the States and seemingly build a different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.340 --> 00:08:28.680
crew. Clearly, Leysa was part of that crew for&nbsp;
a short time. According to ET, Diesel revealed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.680 --> 00:08:33.360
that Cardi B's character will return for the&nbsp;
series' 10th installment, and that she will be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:39.060
expanded on even more. Discussing Leysa's future&nbsp;
in the franchise, "F9" director Justin Lin stated,

00:08:39.060 --> 00:08:42.120
"She's actually really embedded&nbsp;
into the overall universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.120 --> 00:08:46.680
she's been around for a long time and this&nbsp;
is just the first time we are seeing her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.680 --> 00:08:49.740
so I'm really excited to&nbsp;
explore that character of her."

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:55.380
The timeline in the "Furious" franchise can get a&nbsp;
little murky. Although "The Fast and the Furious:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.380 --> 00:08:59.160
Tokyo Drift" is the third film to&nbsp;
have been released in the franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.160 --> 00:09:03.720
it actually takes place as the sixth&nbsp;
in the timeline. This is because Sung&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:09.120
Kang's character Han is introduced and seemingly&nbsp;
killed in "Tokyo Drift." At the end of the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:14.100
Dom appears for a race to honor the friend he&nbsp;
thought he'd lost. At the time, Dom's appearance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:18.840
was a big deal. as this marked the return&nbsp;
of Vin Diesel to the "Furious" franchise.

00:09:18.840 --> 00:09:23.700
As the "Furious" films continued throughout&nbsp;
the years, it's shown that Deckard Shaw is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.700 --> 00:09:28.140
responsible for Han's death in retaliation for&nbsp;
what Dom's crew did to his brother Owen Shaw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.140 --> 00:09:33.780
in "Fast &amp; Furious 6." But wait, there's more!&nbsp;
The plot gets even thicker when it's revealed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.780 --> 00:09:39.300
that Han didn't actually die. Instead, he went&nbsp;
into hiding in order to protect a little girl.

00:09:39.300 --> 00:09:45.000
While the story of Han is a confusing one, there's&nbsp;
a small but entertaining detail about his name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:50.400
that we can't get out of our heads. In "Furious&nbsp;
7," it's revealed that his full name is actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:55.980
Han Seoul-Oh, a clever nod to Harrison Ford's&nbsp;
iconic Star Wars character Han Solo. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.980 --> 00:09:59.760
director Justin Lin later revealed on&nbsp;
Twitter that this is actually just an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:04.200
alias that Han uses, and that his&nbsp;
full name still remains a mystery.

00:10:04.920 --> 00:10:07.447
"No reward is worth this."

00:10:07.740 --> 00:10:13.080
"The Fast and the Furious: Tokyo Drift" introduces&nbsp;
drift racing into the franchise. Up until this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:17.940
point, the series was all about street racing,&nbsp;
with a dash of police drama for good measure.

00:10:17.940 --> 00:10:23.520
"Tokyo Drift" is also the only movie in the&nbsp;
franchise that does not follow Brian or Dominic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:29.160
Instead, an entirely new cast gets introduced. The&nbsp;
film also made the interesting choice to have most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:33.780
of the characters be high school students, rather&nbsp;
than adults taking part in criminal activities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.780 --> 00:10:38.520
In short, it's definitely the most divisive&nbsp;
story to come out of the "Furious" franchise.

00:10:38.520 --> 00:10:42.960
A small scene in the film that holds a lot&nbsp;
of weight is when audiences see a fisherman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.960 --> 00:10:47.520
watching Sean as he embarrassingly attempts&nbsp;
to drift for the first time. The fisherman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:52.740
is actually the real-life "Drift King" Keiichi&nbsp;
Tsuchiya. According to an eagle-eyed Reddit fan,

00:10:52.740 --> 00:10:58.140
"Tsuchiya helped popularize the art and Touge&nbsp;
driving, which also features heavily in the film."

00:10:58.140 --> 00:11:03.540
Surprisingly few professional drivers have&nbsp;
actually made cameos in the Furious franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.540 --> 00:11:08.520
so Tsuchiya's appearance sets "Tokyo Drift"&nbsp;
even further apart from its counterparts.

